      "object": "fig-16-x-unit-label",
      "title": "The x-unit 1 lands on the vertical axis instead of clearly marking x=1",
      "basis": "Actual single native640 image inspection, corroborated by existing SVG text metadata and existing capture metadata; not inferred from render PASS.",
      "detail": "At native640 the horizontal unit glyph sits at the origin/vertical-axis stroke and intersects the horizontal-axis level. The source places this 1 below the x-axis at its first positive grid line. The candidate spec declares at=[1,-0.7], but the SVG emits text-anchor=end at x=656.0761904761905, y=494.55238095238093 with dominant-baseline=middle on its 1280x960 canvas. At the declared half-scale this is an end-anchor at x=328.03809523809525, y=247.27619047619046. Existing capture metadata reports the glyph bounds x=319.1318359375, y=237.41680908203125, width=8.90625, height=17.015625. The y-axis is at x=320 and the horizontal axis at y=243.8095238095238, inside those bounds; the x=1 grid line is x=335.23809523809524. Thus the visually observed crowding is real and obscures the source unit location.",
      "impact": "A learner inspecting the graph can mistake the unit annotation for a mark at the origin rather than a one-unit x scale. The official report checks text/text collisions and minimum text size but does not settle this axis/glyph conflict.",
